{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_09-22-17-omnipaxos-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nCH=\\\"654a1fcfa363488db9f0459d74be6e99-question-0\\\"\ninterp={\\\"investigation_id\\\":CH,\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"handle_majority_promises\\\",\\\"sync_log_accounting\\\",\\\"create_log_sync\\\",\\\"leader_state_promise_meta\\\",\\\"leader_state_max_decided\\\",\\\"no_stopsign_decided_idx\\\",\\\"send_accsync_valid_entries\\\"],\n \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"unresolved\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"unresolved\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Source trace plus a narrowing argument, still without an execution. handle_majority_promises pairs two independently ordered inputs: the log from the promise that is strictly greatest under (n_accepted, accepted_idx) (set_promise replaces max_promise_meta only on a strict Greater; PromiseMetaData's ordering ignores decided_idx) and the decided index from the greatest decided_idx over all recorded promises (get_max_decided_idx); sync_log stores both without clamping, and the leader path has no counterpart to the follower's update_decided_idx_and_get_accepted_idx. For the pairing to break the adopted decided index must exceed the entries the adopted log provides, which requires a promise whose reported decided_idx is greater than the content the leader ends up holding. Narrowing: in the configurations examined here the interposing +1 for a carried StopSign keeps the adopted accepted index at or above the adopted decided index whenever the greatest-accepted promise is the one holding the decided prefix - in the resync/newterm/staleleader runs the followers reported decided 7 with content 6 and the greatest-accepted promise was exactly such a replica, so the leader's post-sync accepted index (sync_idx + suffix + 1) came out at 7 as well and the two matched. A disagreement therefore needs a promise reporting a decided index above its own content while not being the greatest-accepted promise, which none of the observed anomalies produced for the adopted maximum. The suspicion stays unresolved rather than refuted: the protocol argument (a decided entry is accepted by a write quorum intersecting every read quorum, so the greatest-accepted promise should already cover it) is exactly what this code relies on instead of a local bound, and I have neither constructed a reachable disagreement nor bounded it with an execution.\\\"),\n \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"No execution: the account rests on reading handle_majority_promises, set_promise, get_max_decided_idx and sync_log.\\\",\n  \\\"The narrowing rests on the three-node observed runs: the adopted maximum's content was never smaller than the adopted decided index there, partly because the StopSign +1 lifts the adopted accepted index by one in exactly the configuration where a phantom decided index appeared.\\\",\n  \\\"A witness would need a promise whose reported decided_idx exceeds its own content while another promise is the strictly greatest under (n_accepted, accepted_idx); such a history was not constructed.\\\",\n  \\\"Larger clusters, where a promise reporting a high no-StopSign decided index could coexist with a greater-accepted, shorter promise, are untested.\\\"],\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The two orderings coincide in every observed run of this investigation, so no observation supports a disagreement yet.\\\"],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"8dd4ec3c466747dc9a00545e17321baf-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\\\"assertion\\\":\\\"the leader adopts the log and the decided index from two independently ordered inputs\\\"}]}\nup={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Narrow the child question after a closer source analysis, without claiming a witness. The pairing in handle_majority_promises can only break the adopted-decided-covered-by-log relation if some promise reports a decided index above the content the leader ends up holding; in the observed runs the greatest-accepted promise held the decided prefix and the interposed +1 for its carried StopSign lifted the leader's adopted accepted index to the same value as the adopted decided index, so the two matched even where a phantom decided index had appeared. The child interpretation keeps conclusion and defect_claim unresolved, gains this narrowing and its limits, and its discriminator is restricted to histories with a promise reporting a decided index above its own content that is not the greatest-accepted promise.\\\"),\n \\\"investigations\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":CH,\\\"disposition\\\":\\\"active\\\",\n  \\\"discriminator\\\":\\\"A promise that reports a decided index above its own content while another promise is strictly greatest under (n_accepted, accepted_idx); the decisive observation is the newly elected leader's adopted decided index against the entries its adopted log provides immediately after the prepare phase.\\\"}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[interp]}\njson.dump(up, open('update.json','w'), indent=1); print('written')\nPY\ntimeout 120 /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_09-22-17-omnipaxos-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | head -c 900\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
