{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Narrowed the child question by closer source analysis rather than claiming a witness. The pairing in handle_majority_promises (log from the strictly greatest (n_accepted, accepted_idx) promise, decided index from the greatest reported decided index over all promises, no clamp in the leader path) can only break the adopted-decided-covered-by-log relation if some promise reports a decided index above the content the leader ends up holding; in the five observed runs the greatest-accepted promise held the decided prefix and the interposed +1 for its carried StopSign lifted the leader's adopted accepted index to the same value as the adopted decided index (followers at decided 7 with content 6, leader at accepted 7), so the two matched even where a phantom decided index had appeared. The child interpretation keeps conclusion and defect_claim unresolved, now records that narrowing with its limits (no execution; no witness history built; larger clusters where a high no-StopSign decided report could coexist with a greater-accepted shorter promise are untested), states as counterevidence that the two orderings coincided in every observation, and cites the behaviour about the two independently ordered inputs. Its discriminator is restricted to a promise reporting a decided index above its own content that is not the greatest-accepted promise, with the decisive observation being the newly elected leader's adopted decided index against the entries its adopted log provides right after the prepare phase. No execution was requested this turn. Incomplete: that witness history and its probe, the same-lineage follow-ups noted for the parent account (loss- and timer-triggered re-synchronization, larger clusters, persistence across restarts, compaction calls), and any further independent lead.\"}"}}
